{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to The Economist for supporting PBS. \nThe tardigrade, the water bear, the moss piglet,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capable of surviving extreme drying, freezing \nheat, radiation, the vacuum of space,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe even asteroid impact. And who knows, \nmaybe some tardigrades have hitchhiked on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impact ejector to Mars. And according to a new \nstudy, the tardigrade can survive most of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ravages of the Martian surface. Most. When these \nresearchers tried adding a chemical that is both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31760,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abundant on Mars and horrifically toxic, even \nthe tardigrade wounds down. So if Earth's most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40400,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intrepid extreophile can't handle the hostility \nof the red planet, can anything? Well, maybe yes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48560,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe even today. Let's see where Martian life \nand tardigrade astronauts may still be hiding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've got a couple of quick announcements \nbefore we start. First, our eternal battle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the algorithm continues. The best way \nto encourage YouTube to share our videos is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to like and comment. Doing both really makes a \ndifference. And if you're new here, subscribe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hit the bell, and introduce yourself in \nthe comments. We're friendly. Next up,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're excited to launch our black hole light \ncurves desktop and gaming mat. This NASA inspired"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89440,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "32''x16'' mat accurately highlights the ways light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92720,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is warped by the gravity \nof a black hole. The colors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are there to help you interpret how light paths \nare affected by the black hole. There's more info"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the merch store site. Plus, our UV glow cur \nrotating black hole hoodies and shirt plus the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy mugs are all still available. Links in \nthe description. Now, on to the episode. There are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few discoveries that will change everything if \nand when we make them. But one of the biggest is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extraterrestrial life. That's why Mars has been \nsuch a focus for NASA's exploration of the solar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system. It was once so much like the Earth, a \nreal atmosphere, liquid water on the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a while, we thought we might find primitive \nlife cling to existence on that surface. This was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boyed by Viking Landers labeled release experiment \nwhich tentatively detected metabolic byproducts in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Martian soil. But NASA ultimately concluded that \nthis was due to abiotic soil chemistry. And as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inhospitability of the Martian surface became \nclearer, we downgraded our hopes. For some time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now, we've mostly been focused on our searches \nfor signs of past life. And just last year, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166320,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perseverance rover found the most tantalizing such \nsign in the Jezero crater. Mineral composition and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterning that closely resembles the metabolic \nbyproducts of certain Earth microbes. And yes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180560,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we did cover that. This potential bio signature \nis not conclusive, yet it's extremely exciting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188640,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although maybe exciting is now a relative term \nrelative to our diminishing hope of finding actual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "living life on Mars. Now, don't get me wrong, \nsigns of past life would be huge. But what if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're not ready to give up? Maybe the Jezero \ncrater discovery should raise our hopes that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207760,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somewhere the descendants of whatever left those \nmarks are still there, somehow eluding our best"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215520,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "efforts. If so, where might they be? There are \nat least a few regions on Mars that could host"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222480,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some sort of ecosystem. So, let's find them. First \nup, let's review why Mars used to be an oasis and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why it's now a hell hole. A wealth of evidence \nfrom minerals that only form in water to actual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dried river and lake beds showed that liquid \nwater was once abundant on the Martian surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also know that Mars once had a much thicker \natmosphere. Back in the day, Mars also probably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had a magnetic field powered, as with Earth, by a \nnatural dynamo, a solid iron core spinning within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a molten envelope. But Mars is a lot smaller than \nEarth, and it shed its smaller reservoir of heat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more quickly. The core solidified, the magnetic \nfield shut off, and the Martian atmosphere was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exposed to the ablative effect of the solar \nwind. As that atmosphere was sheared away,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperatures dropped, more atmospheric gases were \nfrozen into the surface. As was the water. We see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that water now in Martian ice caps and it seems \noccasionally leaking up from a warmer interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283840,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the surface of Mars is now bone dry with an \nesphixxiatingly thin atmosphere that leaves it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291360,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exposed to intense solar ultraviolet radiation \nand cosmic rays. But it's even worse than that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297920,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The exposed surface is almost all regalith. Dust, \nsand, and rock fragments built up from billions of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years of meteorite impacts amplified by weathering\nand erosion. And with no tectonic activity to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312960,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recycle this stuff, baking under hard UV, this \nregolith is photochemically altered into various"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321280,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "salts of chlorine and sulfur. But without that \ntectonic recycling or dissolution by water, these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328880,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just build up and up and up. Now, many of them, \nlike the perch chlorates, are extremely oxidizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and generally bad news for delicate creatures \nmade from organic molecules. Long story short,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341600,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Martian surface sucks. If we're searching for \nextent life on Mars, we need places that are the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bullseye of a habitability ven diagram. Now, Earth \nboasts some species that are tolerant of some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty extreme circumstances. So, we don't need to \nbe too prescriptive here, but there are some basic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362080,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conditions that are going to be necessary for any \nlife. First, we need liquid water. Now, persistent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large bodies of water may not exist, so we'll need \nto get creative there. Second, we need protection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from all that super bad stuff that Mars tries \nto inflict on life, like the extreme UV and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic rays, the toxic chemicals at the surface, \nand the extreme temperature swings. Third,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need some source of energy. Now, for most life \non Earth, that's ultimately the sun. But on Mars,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direct exposure to the sun means exposure to all \nof the bad things. So, we need to get creative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399840,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here, too. So, we want to come up with a mission \nto search for where these non-negotiables actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overlap. Water, protection, energy. The easiest \nway to deal with the protection issue is to get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below the surface. And essentially, all of our \npossible habitats will be subterranean or sub"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421840,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Martian-ian. The less we have to dig,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425280,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sooner we'll be able to engineer \nour mission. So, let's start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as close to the surface as possible. In fact, we \nonly need to go down a few centimeters into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Martian surface to dramatically reduce ultraviolet \nradiation and a few tens of centime to give some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffer from temperature swings. This doesn't \nfix the other major problems of the surface at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these depths. It's always still below the freezing \npoint of water and we do need liquid water and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hazardous Martian salts remain extremely abundant. \nBut strangely, it turns out that those same salts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459200,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may solve the freezing problem. Salts like \nperchlorates are highly oxidizing, but they're"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also hygroscopic, meaning they can absorb water \nvapor directly from the atmosphere, like those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little packets of silica. They're also powerful \nantifreeze agents, allowing the accumulated water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478320,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to remain a liquid at temperatures as low as \n-70 C. Under the right temperature and humidity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486160,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conditions, these salts can deliquesce. They form \nthin films of brine around particles and within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493360,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gaps of the Martian regolith. These would likely \nform in the Martian night and then evaporate in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the day. So you could imagine primitive life forms \nthat go into stasis, perhaps even dehydrating to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506240,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid the most dangerous oxidation when the UV is \nhigh. But at night they'd activate metabolizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the chemical gradients in the Martian soil. Are \nsuch organisms even plausible? Well, actually,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521280,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yeah. We have things on Earth that do some of the \nrequired things. There are various halophilic or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "salt loving microbes, some of which even use \nperchlorates as a key part of their metabolic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycle. For microbes in the Martian regolith, \nthe sun would be the ultimate source of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a perchlorate metabolizing critter \nwill eat those molecules during the night while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sun's UV will replenish them during the \nday. And this points to a question that we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553600,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "address without even going to Mars. How would \nthe hardiest Earth microbes fare in simulated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Martian conditions? It's not that hard to fake \nthe Martian surface. ultraviolet radiation, low"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566960,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure CO2 atmosphere, cold and a regolith-like \nsoil containing a variety of these salts. Now,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I already mentioned the recent tardigrade study. \nThese things do pretty well until perchlorates are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580880,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "introduced. Then they really suffer. And in a \n2024 study, halaphilic bacteria and fungi were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found to survive pretty well in \nMars-like regolith conditions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593840,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially at several centimeters depth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But again, this was only until the perchlorates \nwere introduced. Then everything died. So on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one hand, perchlorates may be the best of the \nMartian salts at forming brines. They suck in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more water and more critically they suppress \nthe freezing point below the actual Martian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface temperature. But on the other hand, \nthey're lethal. But don't underestimate the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ingenuity of life. Even here on Earth, we have \nmicrobes that can use those perchlorates as a key"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627680,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of their metabolism. And on Mars, that could \nkeep the abundance of this stuff in check. And a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "study just last year found that when you expose \nE.coli to increasing perchlorate concentrations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they activate pathways for DNA repair and stress \nmitigation, showing that there are biological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solutions to the perchlorate toxicity. On Mars, \nthese perchlorates built up relatively slowly over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions of years. And so it's plausible that \nmicro species there have evolved to withstand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661680,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even utilize this stuff. So life could exist \nat shallow depths in the Martian regolith even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669760,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it's a marginal and tortured existence. But \nthe upside is that this stuff is the easiest to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "search for. And because of that our putitive life \nfinding missions are already underway. The Rosalyn"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683920,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Franklin rover. now targeted for a 2028 launch is \ndesigned with a 2 m drill that'll get to depths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where cosmic ray protection is good enough even \nfor humans. The mission is focused on looking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for signs of past life, but it's possible it'll \ndetect biomarkers in the samples that it draws up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It could even be that the Perseverance rover has \nalready dug up lifebearing material from shallower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709920,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depths and packaged it for return to Earth. Now, \nthat sample return mission is now in question due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to budgetary chaos. But if we can get it to a \nproper lab on Earth and take a look for all of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bio signatures, even the subtlest or sparsest \nones that our Mars robots haven't been able to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730320,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catch could be found. Okay, let's see if we can \nfind slightly more hospitable habitats. Mars has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "widespread near surface ground ice, especially \nat high latitudes, and orbital data suggests"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745280,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buried ice deposits at mid latitudes, too. This \nice would solve our protection issues. Water in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid or liquid form is a fantastic UV and cosmic \nray shield. A meter of it is a common shielding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plan for speculative human space missions. And \nthat same UV blocking power and separation from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere slows the chemistry that leads \nto the formation of perchlorates, which is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably a good thing. In either phase, water has \nwonderful temperature stability, which means the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer below this surface ice is protected from the \nwild temperature fluctuations on the surface. Now,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786480,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid water is not liquid water, which is what we \nreally want. But a 2024 NASA study found that melt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water might occasionally form beneath the ground \nice. Now, these would be thin layers of water that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are warmed from below by weak geothermal energy \nand from above by the UV depleted sunlight that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filters through the ice. That liquid phase \nis also helped by the increased pressure from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ice layer and perhaps some lowering of the \nfreezing point from a hopefully not too toxic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "salt concentration. Ice also supports another \nintriguing possibility, radiolysis. Ionizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation interacting with ice can split water \nmolecules producing hydrogen and oxidants. On"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth, some deep subsurface microbes do live off \nhydrogen produced by water rock interactions and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiolysis. The Martian radiation environment \nwhile hostile at the surface could generate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chemical energy in protected icerich regions. \nNow finding this sub ice life would also require"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a shallow drilling mission and the Rosalyn \nFranklin mission may actually find subsurface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ice to investigate. But there's also the Mars Life \nExplorer proposal. If green lit, this would work"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards a mid- latatitude lander that'll aim for \na location with surface ice. It would have a 2 m"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drill just like Rosal and Franklin and onboard \nlabs to look for bio signatures and signs of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876880,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metabolism both in the bore hole and perhaps \nseeping up from layers of Mars deeper down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883920,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another potential habitat is the Martian lava \ntubes. Orbital imagery has revealed skylights,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891440,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapsed ceilings exposing underground voids \nthat likely connect to lava tubes that would have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed billions of years ago when Mars was still \nvolcanically active. These natural caverns could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "provide excellent radiation shielding and thermal \nstability. The great unknown here is whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's water. There could be ice deposits or \nbrines, perhaps even occasional water welling up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from below, or they could be bone dry. In the case \nof lava tubes, we're going to need to send digging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or drilling equipment much heavier than any lander \nor rover can handle. Maybe we just send humans to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explore the labyrinthine Martian underworld. What \ncould go wrong? Anywhere close to the Martian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface, water is probably transient and energy is \nlimited. That means we're unlikely to find dense,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940960,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diverse, or expansive ecosystems. More like \nsparse, scattered communities that may be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947520,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dormant much of the time and metabolize and grow \nat glacial speeds. Just like the Earth microbes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that live in sedimentary layers deep beneath \nthe seafloors with their cell division rates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of hundreds of thousands of years. In the case of \nMars, going deeper should improve habitability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just meters down, there's full protection from \nthe worst ravages of the surface. Deeper still,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature starts to increase. Although \nthe Martian interior is solid to the core,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's still heated by the decay of radioactive \nmaterial. Same as on Earth. Temperature rises"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985120,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by about 10 Kelvin per kilometer. By the time you \nreach about 6 km or so, it should be warm enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for water to be liquid, if there's any water \nthere. But intriguingly, recent work published"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999040,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2024 used seismic data from NASA's Insight \nlander to postulate that parts of the Martian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007360,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "midcrust may contain gigantic aquifers. Extensive \nregions of basically groundwater at depths of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roughly 10 to 20 km. The interpretation of the \nseismic data is still debated, but if confirmed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is potentially the largest region on Mars \nthat could support an ecosystem. The key here is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the seismic data is consistent with aquifers. \nSo, water saturated fractured rock as opposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036880,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to say water carrying clays or solid rock. Water \nneeds to be able to flow in order to not stagnate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any aquous ecosystem will reach chemical and \nenergetic equilibrium unless there's a way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to maintain chemical gradients for energy and to \nreplenish nutrients and to export waste products"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a deep aquifer. That means the fractures \nneed to connect regions over a sufficiently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060720,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large scale. Now we absolutely do have analog life \nfors on Earth, diverse bacteria and archa that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068000,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thrive in deep aquifers persisting on geochemical \nenergy pathways. If Mars has the right geology,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's no reason that such a deep biosphere \nshould not also exist there. The main issue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with this last prospect is that it's going to \nbe enormously difficult to test the idea. It's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be a long, long time before we can put \ntogether a mission to drill several kilometers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beneath the Martian surface. Given the scale \nand complexity of that effort, this might be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something that also has to wait for an actual \nhuman presence. Look, we honestly have no idea"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100400,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether there's currently life on Mars or ever \nwas. But the implications of extent life would be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107680,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so huge that we got to look. Maybe Earth life was \nactually spawned by hitchhikers on impact ejecta"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115280,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from Mars. Or maybe the other way around. Only the \ndiscovery of living life on Mars with its intact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122640,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DNA would tell us that. And if Martian life formed \nindependently of Earth's, well, that tells us that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life really does form easily and often, improving \nthe odds that we'll keep finding weird and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wonderful life forms as we expand our reach across \ninterstellar spacetime. Thank you to The Economist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for supporting PBS. From reporting on scientific \ninnovation to the shifting landscape of global"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "politics, The Economist's mission is to deliver \ncomprehensive coverage that goes beyond the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "headlines. The Economist aims to make difficult \ntopics feel approachable, not daunting, such as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a recent feature about America and China's return \nto the moon and the future of lunar exploration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A digital subscription to The Economist includes \ndaily journalism, plus access to their digital"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weekly edition, subscriberonly podcasts \nand newsletters, and Insider, a new weekly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video series where senior editors debate the \nforces shaping geopolitics, defense, economics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182000,
    "dDurationMs": 10880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and technology. As a viewer of PBS Spacetime, you \ncan get a special 35% off to enjoy The Economist."
    } ]
  } ]
}
